  "answer_text": "Here's a comprehensive breakdown of what mutual fund managers charge and earn as a percentage:\n\n---\n\n## \ud83d\udcbc Mutual Fund Manager Fees (%)\n\n### \ud83d\udcca Overall Range\nManagement fees, whether paid as a mutual fund expense ratio or a fee paid to a financial advisor, **typically range from 0.01% to over 2%**.\n\nManagement fees can vary from manager to manager and financial firm to financial firm, but are commonly a **percentage of the total assets under management (AUM)**.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udd0d Breakdown by Fund Type\n\n#### \u2705 Actively Managed Funds\nOperating fees are typically **between 0.25% and 1%** of your total investment per year. If the fund is actively managed to try to beat average stock market returns, these costs are higher than for passively managed funds.\n\n#### \ud83d\udcc9 Passively Managed Funds (Index Funds/ETFs)\nPassively managed funds, such as index funds and ETFs, aim to mirror the performance of a specific stock index. They involve less manual intervention and **typically have lower fees**.\n\n#### \ud83c\udfe6 Wrap Fees (All-in-One)\nSometimes an investment manager will consolidate a client's various fees into what is called a **wrap fee**. Such a fee may encompass the management of both retirement and non-retirement accounts, financial advice and planning services, brokerage services, and other fund fees. Investment managers charge wrap fees at **1% to 3%** of the assets they manage for you.\n\n---\n\n### \u2699\ufe0f What Drives Higher or Lower Fees?\n\n- More **aggressive investment portfolios** tend to have higher management fees because there is more work involved due to the higher turnover of securities.\n- Mutual funds and ETFs typically have **lower management fees** when compared with hedge funds or private equity funds, which may charge higher fees due to their complex investment strategies and higher potential returns.\n- Some funds also use **performance-based fees**, where managers earn additional compensation based on the fund's performance.\n\n---\n\n### \u26a0\ufe0f Why Fees Matter\nAn expense ratio of 0.75% versus 0.10% can result in a **difference of thousands of dollars** over 30 years \u2014 particularly in retirement accounts like a 401(k) or Roth IRA.\n\nA fund with high costs **must perform better** than a low-cost fund to generate the same returns for you.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udcdd Quick Summary Table\n\n| Fund Type | Typical Fee Range |\n|---|---|\n| Index Funds / ETFs | 0.01% \u2013 0.20% |\n| Actively Managed Funds | 0.25% \u2013 1%+ |\n| High-Complexity/Niche Funds | Up to 2%+ |\n| Wrap Fees | 1% \u2013 3% |\n\nThe key takeaway: **always check the expense ratio** before investing, as even small percentage differences can significantly impact your long-term returns.",
